</table>
</div>
<p>On the flip-side, if you happen to be a funder thinking about how to start this process with your grantees:</p>
<ol>
<li aria-level="1">Thank you for saving the world, and</li>
<li aria-level="1">Have patience. The nonprofits you work with are used to operating in a system that penalizes them for being up-front about their indirect costs. Indeed, they may have so internalized the penny-pinching, “get it done” approach to nonprofit work that <a href="https://ssir.org/up_for_debate/article/pay_what_it_takes_philanthropy" rel="noopener"><span>they don’t actually&nbsp;<em>know&nbsp;</em>what their real indirect costs are! </span></a>It’ll take time to build up trust with organizations that have been burned for their transparency before. Make your commitment to your grantees clear, again and again, and eventually you’ll get a fuller picture of what’s actually going on, and how you can help.</li>
</ol>
<p>Change won’t come easily, or quickly. But with dedication, honesty, and trust, we can all work toward a world where nonprofit organizations have the resources they need to serve their clients—<em>and </em>give their employees the resources and compensation they deserve.</p></span>
                        
                    </div>
                </div>
            </article>
        </div>
        

        
        

